WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.150
A signed offer is the start of a working relationship.

2
00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:10.272
Onboarding helps a new employee understand the institution, colleagues, expectations, and practical arrangements.

3
00:00:10.472 --> 00:00:12.945
Orientation is part of that process.

4
00:00:13.145 --> 00:00:16.693
Training develops knowledge and skills needed for the current job.

5
00:00:16.893 --> 00:00:21.754
Staff development builds broader capacity for growth and future responsibilities.

6
00:00:21.954 --> 00:00:30.112
Cedar Crossing teaches a new assistant how to check registration records, demonstrates the process, and provides guided practice with feedback.

7
00:00:30.312 --> 00:00:31.425
That is training.

8
00:00:31.625 --> 00:00:38.019
Later, observing how a colleague designs an accessible workshop could support development toward a wider role.

9
00:00:38.219 --> 00:00:41.766
The activities may overlap; the purpose helps distinguish them.

10
00:00:41.966 --> 00:00:45.944
A course certificate alone does not show that someone can apply the skill.

11
00:00:46.144 --> 00:00:50.415
Check performance in realistic conditions and ask what support is missing.

12
00:00:50.615 --> 00:00:54.431
Time, usable instructions, and opportunities to practice matter.

13
00:00:54.631 --> 00:01:02.916
Mentoring can help someone interpret the workplace and explore a career, but access should not depend solely on finding a well-connected colleague.
